## 3. The Three Input Sources — Explained in Detail

### 3.1 The Daily Log File

**Path pattern:** `<line's log directory>\<yyyymmdd>_New.log`
Example: `C:\InLine_Pro\logs\LINE01\20260818_New.log`

Each line has **its own log folder** and its own file per day. A new file is created every day; the system reads it every 10 minutes throughout the day.

**Relevant log line format:**
```
[ 0:00:25], [Trace Log], [Rear]ScanSendPoint: A6706153AFA59
[10:38:15], [Trace Log], [Front]ScanSendPoint: A6713079AFYB2
```

What we extract from each line:
| Part | Meaning |
|---|---|
| `[ 0:00:25]` | Time-of-day (H:MM:SS or HH:MM:SS) — **no date** is present on most lines |
| `[Rear]` or `[Front]` | Which rack side scanned a board |
| `ScanSendPoint:` | The literal event marker we look for |
| `A6706153AFA59` | The scanned board's ID (not used for status, only the fact that a scan happened matters) |

**Why the date isn't in the log line:** the file is date-stamped only in its **filename**. The code (`log_parser.py`) derives the date from the filename (`20260818` → 18-Aug-2026) and combines it with each line's time-of-day to build a full timestamp.

**The 45-minute liveness rule:** the system finds the single most recent `ScanSendPoint` timestamp anywhere in the file (Front or Rear combined — confirmed this is a whole-line check, not separate per side). If "now" is within 45 minutes of that timestamp, the line is **RUNNING**. If more than 45 minutes have passed with no new `ScanSendPoint`, the line is **STALE** (effectively stopped/idle).

### 3.2 The Data.ini File

**Path:** one per line, wherever the Excel line master says (`Config File / Directory` column).

**Relevant structure:**
```ini
[RACK1]
BUILDER1 = CHECK
BUILDER2 = CHECK
BUILDER3 = CHECK
BUILDER4 = NOT_CHECK
...
BUILDER10 = CHECK
FUNCTION1 = CHECK
FUNCTION2 = CHECK
FUNCTION3 = CHECK
FUNCTION4 = CHECK

[RACK2]
BUILDER1 .. BUILDER10 = CHECK / NOT_CHECK
FUNCTION1 .. FUNCTION4 = CHECK / NOT_CHECK
```

`CHECK` means that DL/FT unit is currently **Running**. `NOT_CHECK` means it's currently **Stopped**.

**Mapping to the global DL/FT numbering scheme used throughout the app:**

| Global number | Maps to |
|---|---|
| DL 1–10 | RACK1 (Front) BUILDER1–10 |
| DL 11–20 | RACK2 (Rear) BUILDER1–10 (i.e. DL14 = RACK2 BUILDER4) |
| FT 1–4 | RACK1 (Front) FUNCTION1–4 |
| FT 5–8 | RACK2 (Rear) FUNCTION1–4 (i.e. FT6 = RACK2 FUNCTION2) |

This mapping is implemented in `data_ini_parser.py`'s `dl_status()` and `ft_status()` functions.

### 3.3 The Excel Line Master

**One row per line.** Expected columns (header row 1, matched case-insensitively — see `line_master.py` for exact alias list):

| Column | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Line No. | Unique line identifier, e.g. `LINE 01` |
| Line Name | Display name, e.g. `Assembly Line 01` |
| IP Address | The line's network address (currently informational only) |
| Data Directory (Path) | Folder containing that line's daily log files |
| Config File / Directory | Full path to that line's `Data.ini` |
| DL | **Total DL setup** — how many of the 20 possible DL slots physically exist on this line |
| FT | **Total FT setup** — how many of the 8 possible FT slots physically exist on this line |
| Status | `Enabled` / `Disabled` — whether this line should be polled at all |

**Why "Total DL/FT" matters — the slot-trim rule:**

A line doesn't necessarily have all 20 DL units or all 8 FT units installed. The Excel `Total DL` / `Total FT` values tell us how many actually exist — and the missing ones are **always the highest-numbered slots of each rack** (confirmed with the factory team):

```
Total DL = 18  (missing 2, split evenly = 1 per rack)
  → DL10 (last slot of RACK1) and DL20 (last slot of RACK2) don't exist
  → Valid DL slots: 1-9, 11-19

Total DL = 16  (missing 4, split evenly = 2 per rack)
  → DL9, DL10, DL19, DL20 don't exist
  → Valid DL slots: 1-8, 11-18

Total FT = 6  (missing 2, split evenly = 1 per rack)
  → FT4 and FT8 don't exist
  → Valid FT slots: 1-3, 5-7
```

This is implemented in `slot_rules.py`. **Important distinction:** a slot excluded by this rule is *not shown at all* (it doesn't exist) — this is different from a slot that exists but is `NOT_CHECK` in Data.ini (which *is* shown, as "Stopped").

If a line's shortfall is odd (can't be split evenly across 2 racks), the code deliberately raises an error rather than guessing — that line would need a real per-slot exclusion list instead of the computed rule, and should be flagged for review rather than silently mis-reported.

---

## 4. Business Logic — How a Line's Status Is Decided

This is the full decision sequence the engine runs for **every line, every 10 minutes** (`status_engine.py` → `compute_line_report()`):

```
1. Is the line marked "Disabled" in Excel?
       YES → status = DISABLED. Stop here. (Not polled further.)
       NO  → continue

2. Look for today's log file: <log_dir>/<yyyymmdd>_New.log
       File missing or unreadable → status = ERROR (with the reason recorded).
                                     Stop here.
       File found → continue

3. Find the most recent ScanSendPoint timestamp in the file.
       No ScanSendPoint events found at all → status = STALE. Stop here.
       Gap between "now" and that timestamp > 45 minutes → status = STALE. Stop here.
       Gap ≤ 45 minutes → status = RUNNING. Continue.

4. (Only reached if RUNNING) Compute valid DL slots and valid FT slots
   from Excel's Total DL / Total FT (the slot-trim rule, section 3.3).

5. (Only reached if RUNNING) Read the line's Data.ini.
       File missing or unreadable → keep status = RUNNING, but record the
                                     error and leave dl_report/ft_report empty.
       File found → for every valid slot, look up CHECK/NOT_CHECK →
                     Running/Stopped, and build the DL/FT report.
```

**Why STALE/ERROR don't compute DL/FT status:** if the line itself isn't running (or we can't even tell), reporting individual DL/FT statuses would be misleading — those numbers are only meaningful while the line is actively producing. The web UI reflects this by showing an empty state for DL/FT when a line isn't RUNNING.

**Shift definitions** (`shifts.py`):

| Shift | Window |
|---|---|
| A | 07:00 – 15:00 (same calendar day) |
| B | 15:00 – 23:00 (same calendar day) |
| C | 23:00 – 07:00 (**spans into the next calendar day**) |

For Shift C, "date" always refers to the day the shift **starts** — e.g. "Shift C on 2026-08-18" means 23:00 on the 18th through 07:00 on the 19th. This matches how a factory floor conventionally refers to "last night's C shift."

---

## 5. Module-by-Module Reference

### `app/parsers/log_parser.py`
- `parse_scan_send_points(file_path)` — streams a log file line-by-line (memory-efficient even for large files — doesn't load the whole file into RAM) and returns a list of `ScanEvent(timestamp, side, board_id)` objects.
- `compute_line_status(events, as_of, threshold_minutes=45)` — applies the 45-minute rule and returns `LineStatusResult(status, last_seen, gap_minutes)`.
- Handles the log's quirk of only having time-of-day (no date) by deriving the base date from the **filename**, and defensively rolls the date forward if it ever detects time going backwards by more than a minute (in case a log file happens to span midnight).

### `app/parsers/data_ini_parser.py`
- `parse_data_ini(file_path)` — reads the RACK1/RACK2 sections and returns a `DataIniStatus` object holding `{builder_number: is_running}` and `{function_number: is_running}` dictionaries for each rack.
- `dl_status(data, dl_num)` / `ft_status(data, ft_num)` — apply the global-number-to-rack mapping (section 3.2) and return `True` (Running) / `False` (Stopped). Raises `KeyError` if that slot isn't present in the file at all (handled gracefully upstream — treated as Stopped).

### `app/engine/slot_rules.py`
- `valid_dl_slots(total_dl)` / `valid_ft_slots(total_ft)` — implement the slot-trim rule (section 3.3). Pure functions — same input always gives same output, easy to unit test (see `tests/test_engine.py`).

### `app/engine/shifts.py`
- `shift_window(date, shift)` — returns `(start_datetime, end_datetime)` for a shift, correctly handling Shift C's midnight rollover.
- `parse_date(date_str)` — parses a `YYYY-MM-DD` string.

### `app/engine/status_engine.py`
- `compute_line_report(line, as_of=None)` — the orchestrator described in section 4. Returns a `LineStatusReport` dataclass with computed properties like `dl_running_count`, `dl_stopped_count`, and a `.to_dict()` method for JSON/DB serialization.
- Deliberately **never raises** for expected operational issues (missing files) — it converts them into an `ERROR` status with the reason recorded, so one line's problem never crashes the whole polling cycle.

### `app/config/line_master.py`
- `load_line_master(xlsx_path)` — reads the Excel workbook via `openpyxl`, matches column headers tolerantly (see `HEADER_ALIASES` — handles minor wording/spacing differences), and returns a list of `LineConfig` objects.

### `app/db/database.py` and `app/db/repository.py`
- Uses **SQLAlchemy Core** (not raw SQL, not an ORM) so the exact same code works against SQLite (used here for dev/testing) and SQL Server 2014 (production) — only the `DATABASE_URL` environment variable changes.
- `repository.py` provides: `save_snapshot()`, `latest_snapshot_per_line()`, `latest_snapshot_for_line()`, `history_for_line()`, `history_all_lines()`.
- DL/FT reports are stored as plain JSON-encoded text (`NVARCHAR`), not a native JSON column type — this is deliberate, because SQL Server 2014 predates native JSON support (added in 2016).

### `app/scheduler/poller.py`
- `poll_once(xlsx_path, as_of=None)` — loads the Excel line master, computes a report for every line, and saves each as a snapshot. One line failing unexpectedly is logged and skipped — it doesn't stop the rest of the cycle.
- `start_scheduler(xlsx_path)` — starts an APScheduler `BackgroundScheduler` that calls `poll_once` every 10 minutes, with an immediate first run on startup.

### `app/export.py`
- `_flatten(snapshots)` — converts nested snapshot rows (one row per line per cycle, with DL/FT as embedded JSON) into a flat table: one row per DL/FT slot per cycle. This is the shape used both for CSV/XLSX downloads and the Historical Data web page.
- `to_csv_bytes()` / `to_xlsx_bytes()` — generate the actual downloadable file content.

### `app/api/routes.py` (JSON API)
See section 11 for the full endpoint reference.

### `app/api/html_routes.py` (Web pages)
See section 10 for the full page reference. Built with **zero JavaScript** per requirement — all filtering, pagination, and downloads work through plain HTML forms/links and full page reloads.

### `app/main.py`
The FastAPI application entrypoint — mounts static files, includes both routers, and wires up the DB init + scheduler start on application startup.

---

## 6. Database Schema

Single table: `line_status`. One row is written **per line, per poll cycle** (i.e. every 10 minutes, every line gets a new row — this is intentionally a time-series log, not an upsert, so full history is preserved).

| Column | Type | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| id | Integer, PK, autoincrement | (`IDENTITY` on SQL Server) |
| line_no | String(50) | e.g. `LINE 01` |
| line_name | String(200) | e.g. `Assembly Line 01` |
| ip_address | String(50) | |
| as_of | String(30) | ISO 8601 timestamp of this poll cycle |
| line_status | String(20) | `RUNNING` \| `STALE` \| `ERROR` \| `DISABLED` |
| last_seen | String(30) | ISO 8601, last ScanSendPoint timestamp seen |
| gap_minutes | Float | Minutes between `as_of` and `last_seen` |
| total_dl / total_ft | Integer | From Excel, at time of this cycle |
| dl_running / dl_stopped | Integer | Counts, for quick dashboard display |
| ft_running / ft_stopped | Integer | Counts, for quick dashboard display |
| dl_report_json / ft_report_json | String(4000) | JSON text, e.g. `{"1": true, "2": false, ...}` — plain text, not a native JSON column (SQL Server 2014 compatibility) |
| error | String(1000) | Populated only for ERROR status, or partial errors during a RUNNING cycle |

Indexes: `(line_no, as_of)` and `(as_of)` — supports both "give me this line's history" and "give me everyone's status at time X" query patterns efficiently.

---

## 7. Installation Guide

### Prerequisites
- Python 3.10+ (tested with 3.12)
- Access to the factory's `C:\InLine_Pro\logs\...` and Data.ini paths (read access is sufficient)
- For production: SQL Server 2014 instance + "ODBC Driver 17 for SQL Server" installed on the app server

### Steps

```bash
# 1. Extract the project and enter the folder
cd inline_pro

# 2. Create and activate a virtual environment
python3 -m venv venv
source venv/bin/activate          # Windows: venv\Scripts\activate

# 3. Install dependencies
pip install fastapi uvicorn openpyxl apscheduler python-multipart jinja2 sqlalchemy

# (Production only, for SQL Server 2014 — see section 13)
pip install pyodbc
```

No further build step is needed — this is a pure Python application, no compilation, no Node/npm, no JavaScript bundling.

---

## 8. Configuration Guide

### 8.1 Point the app at your real Excel line master

```bash
# Linux/Mac
export LINE_MASTER_XLSX_PATH=/path/to/line_master.xlsx

# Windows PowerShell
$env:LINE_MASTER_XLSX_PATH="C:\InLine_Pro\config\line_master.xlsx"
```

If not set, it defaults to the bundled test fixture (`tests/fixtures/line_master.xlsx`) — fine for trying the app out, **must** be changed before real use.

Your Excel file must have header row 1 with (some tolerance for wording — see `HEADER_ALIASES` in `line_master.py`):
`Line No.` | `Line Name` | `IP Address` | `Data Directory (Path)` | `Config File / Directory` | `DL` | `FT` | `Status`

### 8.2 Point the app at your real database (production)

See section 13 for the full SQL Server 2014 setup. For local testing, no configuration is needed — SQLite is used automatically, stored at `data/inline_pro.db`.

### 8.3 Verify each line's paths are reachable

Before relying on the dashboard, it's worth manually confirming:
- `<Data Directory Path>\<today's date>_New.log` exists and is readable for each line
- `<Config File / Directory>` (the Data.ini path) exists and is readable for each line

If either is wrong, that line will show status `ERROR` on the dashboard with the exact path and reason in the `error` field — check that field first when troubleshooting a line that never shows RUNNING.

---

## 9. Running the Application

### Development / testing

```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --reload --port 8000
```

- `--reload` restarts the server automatically when you edit code — useful during development, **remove this flag in production**.
- Visit `http://localhost:8000/dashboard` in a browser for the web UI.
- Visit `http://localhost:8000/docs` for interactive, auto-generated API documentation (Swagger UI) — lets you try every JSON endpoint directly from the browser.

### What happens on startup
1. `init_db()` runs — creates the `line_status` table if it doesn't already exist (does **not** create the database itself — that must exist beforehand, especially on SQL Server).
2. The background scheduler starts and immediately runs one poll cycle (so you don't have to wait 10 minutes to see initial data), then repeats every 10 minutes for as long as the process runs.

### Production (Windows server)

Recommended: run behind a process manager so it restarts automatically on crash/reboot. Two common options:

**Option A — NSSM (Non-Sucking Service Manager)**, wraps the command as a Windows Service:
```
nssm install InLineProMonitoring "C:\path\to\venv\Scripts\uvicorn.exe" "app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000"
```

**Option B — Task Scheduler**, run at startup:
Create a scheduled task that runs `venv\Scripts\uvicorn.exe app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000` at system startup, with "Run whether user is logged on or not."

`--host 0.0.0.0` (instead of the default `127.0.0.1`) is needed if other machines on the factory network need to view the dashboard, not just the server itself.

---

## 10. Web Pages — User Guide

All pages are pure HTML/CSS — no JavaScript anywhere. Filtering and pagination work through standard links and form submissions (full page reload each time).

| Page | URL | What it shows |
|---|---|---|
| Dashboard | `/dashboard` (also `/`) | Latest status of every line: summary counts (Running/Stale/Error/Disabled) plus a card per line with DL/FT running counts. Click any line card to see its detail. |
| Lines list | `/lines` | Same data as the dashboard, in a flat table — useful for scanning many lines at once. |
| Line Detail | `/lines/{line_no}` | Full detail for one line: status, last-seen time, gap in minutes, and a color-coded grid of every valid DL and FT slot (green = Running, red = Stopped). If the line isn't currently RUNNING, the DL/FT grids show an empty-state message instead (matches the business rule in section 4). |
| Configuration | `/configuration` | Read-only view of the Excel line master — every line's IP, log path, ini path, DL/FT totals, and enabled/disabled status. To change configuration, edit the Excel file directly; changes take effect on the next poll cycle (no restart needed). |
| Historical Data | `/historical?date=YYYY-MM-DD&shift=A\|B\|C&line_no=...` | Pick a date and shift (and optionally one line) via the filter form, then view a flattened table of every DL/FT status recorded during that shift window, with pagination (50 rows/page) and **Download CSV** / **Download Excel** buttons. |

**Downloading shift data:** on the Historical Data page, after selecting a date/shift (and optional line), two buttons appear. These are plain links straight to the export API (`/api/reports/shift-download`) — clicking them downloads the file directly, no page navigation needed.

---

## 11. API Reference

Base URL: `http://<server>:8000`

| Method | Path | Description |
|---|---|---|
| GET | `/health` | Health check — returns `{"status": "ok"}` |
| GET | `/api/lines` | Dashboard data: summary counts + latest snapshot for every line |
| GET | `/api/lines/{line_no}` | Latest snapshot for one line. `404` if that line has no data yet. |
| GET | `/api/lines/{line_no}/history?start=ISO&end=ISO` | All snapshots for one line within a datetime range |
| GET | `/api/history?start=ISO&end=ISO` | All snapshots for all lines within a datetime range |
| GET | `/api/reports/shift-download?date=YYYY-MM-DD&shift=A\|B\|C&fmt=csv\|xlsx[&line_no=...]` | Shift-wise export. Returns the file directly (`Content-Disposition: attachment`). `404` if no data found for that window; `400` if `shift` isn't A/B/C. |
| POST | `/api/poll-now` | Triggers an on-demand poll cycle across all lines immediately (outside the normal 10-minute schedule) |

**Example — fetch dashboard data:**
```bash
curl http://localhost:8000/api/lines
```

**Example — download Shift C for a specific date and line, as Excel:**
```bash
curl -o shift_data.xlsx "http://localhost:8000/api/reports/shift-download?date=2026-08-18&shift=C&line_no=LINE%2001&fmt=xlsx"
```

Full interactive documentation (try-it-yourself, generated automatically from the code): `http://localhost:8000/docs`

---

## 12. Testing Guide

Every module has a corresponding test file in `tests/`, and they were all run against your **real uploaded log file and Data.ini sample** — not synthetic data — before being trusted.

```bash
cd inline_pro
venv/bin/python tests/test_log_parser.py       # log parsing + 45-min rule
venv/bin/python tests/test_engine.py           # slot-trim rule + Data.ini mapping
venv/bin/python tests/test_status_engine.py    # full per-line status computation
venv/bin/python tests/test_shifts.py           # shift window math (incl. Shift C midnight rollover)
venv/bin/python tests/test_full_pipeline.py    # DB + multi-cycle history + export
venv/bin/python tests/test_api.py              # every JSON API endpoint
venv/bin/python tests/test_html_pages.py       # every HTML page renders correctly
```

Each script prints its results and asserts expected values — a clean run with no `AssertionError` and ending in an "ALL ... PASSED" message means that layer is working correctly. If you change any business rule (e.g., the 45-minute threshold, or the slot-trim logic), re-run these before deploying — they'll catch a regression immediately.

**Test fixtures used** (`tests/fixtures/`):
- `20260818_New.log` — your real uploaded log file
- `Data.ini` — reconstructed from your real pasted sample
- `line_master.xlsx` — a small synthetic Excel file (4 lines, covering RUNNING, ERROR, and DISABLED cases) since a real Excel file hasn't been provided yet

**Before going live**, replace `line_master.xlsx` testing with your actual Excel file and re-run `test_status_engine.py`-style checks against it to confirm every line's paths resolve correctly.

---

## 13. Deploying to SQL Server 2014

The application defaults to SQLite (zero-config, good for testing). For production against your company's SQL Server 2014:

### Step 1 — Create the database (once, in SQL Server Management Studio)
```sql
CREATE DATABASE InLineProDB;
```
(The application creates the `line_status` **table** automatically on startup — it does not create the database itself.)

### Step 2 — Install the ODBC driver on the app server
Download and install "**ODBC Driver 17 for SQL Server**" from Microsoft (compatible with SQL Server 2014).

### Step 3 — Install the Python driver
```bash
pip install pyodbc
```

### Step 4 — Set the connection string

```powershell
# SQL Server authentication
$env:DATABASE_URL="mssql+pyodbc://sql_user:sql_password@YOUR_SQL_SERVER/InLineProDB?driver=ODBC+Driver+17+for+SQL+Server"

# OR Windows integrated authentication
$env:DATABASE_URL="mssql+pyodbc://YOUR_SQL_SERVER/InLineProDB?driver=ODBC+Driver+17+for+SQL+Server&trusted_connection=yes"
```

### Step 5 — Run the app as normal
```bash
uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```
No code changes are required — the same `database.py`/`repository.py` code runs against either database via SQLAlchemy Core.

### Why SQL Server 2014 specifically needed care
- **No native JSON type or JSON functions** (`OPENJSON`, `JSON_VALUE`) — those were added in SQL Server 2016. The DL/FT per-slot reports are stored as plain `NVARCHAR(4000)` text, JSON-encoded/decoded in Python — this works identically on 2014, 2016+, and SQLite.
- **IDENTITY vs AUTOINCREMENT** — handled transparently by SQLAlchemy based on which database dialect it's talking to; no manual SQL needed.

### If `pyodbc` can't be installed
Some corporate environments restrict compiled C extensions. `pymssql` is an alternative pure-Python-friendlier driver — if `pyodbc` is blocked, let the development team know and the connection layer can be adjusted to support a `mssql+pymssql://...` URL instead (same SQLAlchemy Core code, different driver).

**Important — this configuration is untested against a live SQL Server instance.** It was verified as *correct by construction* (valid SQLAlchemy dialect, IDENTITY-compatible schema, no JSON-type dependency), but no SQL Server 2014 instance was available during development. **The first deployment should include a smoke test**: run `tests/test_full_pipeline.py` (with `DATABASE_URL` pointed at the real SQL Server instance) as an early sanity check before relying on it in production.

---

## 14. Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Likely cause | Where to check |
|---|---|---|
| A line always shows `ERROR` | Log directory path or Data.ini path wrong/unreachable | The `error` field on that line's detail page or API response gives the exact path and OS error |
| A line shows `STALE` even though it's running | The log file's `ScanSendPoint` events have stopped appearing, or the log file naming doesn't match `yyyymmdd_New.log` | Manually check the log file's most recent `ScanSendPoint` timestamp; confirm filename matches today's date |
| Dashboard is empty / no lines shown | No poll cycle has run yet, or Excel line master path is misconfigured | Check server startup logs for the immediate first poll; verify `LINE_MASTER_XLSX_PATH` is set correctly |
| A line's DL/FT counts look wrong (too many/few slots) | Total DL/Total FT in Excel doesn't match what's actually wired for that line, or the shortfall is unevenly split across racks | Check that line's `Total DL`/`Total FT` in Excel; the slot-trim rule assumes an even split — an odd shortfall raises an error rather than guessing |
| Shift-wise download returns "No data found" (404) | No poll cycles fell within that shift's time window for that date | Confirm the scheduler was actually running during that time window; check `/api/history` for that date range directly |
| Server won't start: `ModuleNotFoundError` | A dependency isn't installed in the active virtual environment | Re-run the `pip install` commands from section 7 inside the activated venv |
| SQL Server connection fails | ODBC driver not installed, wrong server name, or firewall blocking the connection | Test the raw ODBC connection independently (e.g. via `sqlcmd` or SSMS) before troubleshooting the Python side |

---

## 15. Project File Structure

```
inline_pro/
├── app/
│   ├── parsers/
│   │   ├── log_parser.py         # ScanSendPoint extraction + 45-min liveness rule
│   │   └── data_ini_parser.py    # RACK1/RACK2 CHECK/NOT_CHECK -> DL/FT status
│   ├── engine/
│   │   ├── slot_rules.py         # Total DL/FT -> valid slot numbers (trim rule)
│   │   ├── shifts.py             # Shift A/B/C window calculation
│   │   └── status_engine.py      # Combines all of the above into one report per line
│   ├── config/
│   │   └── line_master.py        # Excel line-master reader
│   ├── db/
│   │   ├── database.py           # SQLAlchemy engine/schema (SQLite or SQL Server)
│   │   └── repository.py         # save/query snapshots
│   ├── scheduler/
│   │   └── poller.py             # 10-minute polling job (APScheduler)
│   ├── api/
│   │   ├── routes.py             # JSON API endpoints
│   │   └── html_routes.py        # HTML page routes (pure HTML/CSS, no JS)
│   ├── templates/                # Jinja2 HTML templates
│   │   ├── base.html
│   │   ├── dashboard.html
│   │   ├── lines.html
│   │   ├── line_detail.html
│   │   ├── configuration.html
│   │   └── historical.html
│   ├── static/
│   │   └── style.css             # All styling — zero JavaScript in the project
│   ├── export.py                 # CSV/XLSX generation for shift downloads
│   ├── main.py                   # FastAPI app entrypoint
│   └── settings.py                # Config (Excel path from env var)
├── tests/                          # One test file per module, run with venv/bin/python
│   └── fixtures/                    # Real log/Data.ini samples + synthetic Excel file
├── docs/
│   └── MANUAL.md                     # This document
├── data/
│   └── inline_pro.db                  # SQLite DB (dev only; not used when DATABASE_URL points to SQL Server)
└── README.md                            # Quick-start summary

```

---

## 16. Known Limitations / Not Yet Built

- **Pass Count / Fail Count per DL/FT** (shown in early UI mockups) is not implemented — it requires parsing PASS/FAIL result lines from the log file, and a sample of that log format hasn't been provided yet. Everything else (line status, DL/FT running/stopped, shift-wise export) is fully working.
- **Pass vs Fail trend charts** were explicitly descoped by request (no JavaScript charting library used).
- **Authentication/Login, Users, and Alerts pages** are not yet built (planned as Phase 5).
- **Editing configuration through the web UI** is not implemented — the Configuration page is currently read-only; changes must be made directly in the Excel file (picked up automatically on the next poll cycle, no restart needed).
- The SQL Server 2014 connection path is implemented and structurally verified, but has not been tested against a live SQL Server instance (see section 13's note).

